### Synapse MinimalApi — Pipeline Behavior Feature Tour
###
### This file demonstrates all four pipeline-behavior scenarios:
###
###   1. ORDERING      — MetricsBehavior(Order=10) wraps AuditBehavior(Order=20).
###                      Look for the ▶/◀ brackets in stdout on every request.
###
###   2. CONSTRAINT    — AuditBehavior only fires on IAuditableRequest commands.
###                      Compare a CreateTask (📝 AUDIT lines present) with a
###                      ListTasks (📝 AUDIT lines absent).
###
###   3. SHORT-CIRCUIT — AuthorizationBehavior returns Result.Failure without ever
###                      calling next().  The 🧹 PURGING handler log only appears
###                      when the X-User-Permissions: tasks:admin header is present.
###
###   4. STREAM        — StreamLoggingBehavior wraps IAsyncEnumerable<Result<T>>
###                      and logs "🔢 Streamed N items" after the stream ends.
###                      (See Http/streaming.http for a dedicated stream demo.)
###
### Run `dotnet run` in examples/MinimalApi, then execute requests top-to-bottom
### while watching the terminal output.
@baseUrl = http://localhost:5225

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Setup — create tasks needed for this demo
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Create task A (used to demonstrate audit trail + later completed for purge demo)
POST {{baseUrl}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Pipeline demo task A",
  "description": "Will be completed and then purged"
}

> {%
    client.test("Task A created", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 201, "Expected 201 Created");
        const location = response.headers.valueOf("Location");
        const guidPattern = /\/tasks\/([0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12})$/i;
        client.global.set("pbTaskIdA", location.match(guidPattern)[1]);
    });
%}

###

### Create task B (kept pending; verifying it survives the purge)
POST {{baseUrl}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Pipeline demo task B",
  "description": "Stays pending — should survive the purge"
}

> {%
    client.test("Task B created", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 201, "Expected 201 Created");
        const location = response.headers.valueOf("Location");
        const guidPattern = /\/tasks\/([0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12})$/i;
        client.global.set("pbTaskIdB", location.match(guidPattern)[1]);
    });
%}

###

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Scenario 1 + 2: Ordering & Constraint-based behavior
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [Feature: Ordering + Constraint] Create a task — observe command pipeline in stdout
###
### Expected stdout (behaviors run outermost first):
###   ▶ [metrics:10] CreateTaskCommand started      ← MetricsBehavior (Order=10, source-gen)
###   📝 [audit:20] CreateTaskCommand — pipeline entry recorded  ← AuditBehavior (Order=20, source-gen)
###   info: ... CreateTaskCommand handled in ...    ← SimpleLoggingBehavior (runtime)
###   info: Creating task: Pipeline demo task A     ← handler
###   📝 [audit:20] CreateTaskCommand succeeded     ← AuditBehavior exit
###   ◀ [metrics:10] CreateTaskCommand finished     ← MetricsBehavior exit
###
### Note: AuditBehavior appears because CreateTaskCommand implements IAuditableRequest.
### The source generator's Satisfies() check cross-products AuditBehavior<,> with
### CreateTaskCommand at compile time (see RegisterGroup.g.cs in obj/).
POST {{baseUrl}}/tasks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Ordering demo task",
  "description": "Watch the stdout bracket nesting"
}

> {%
    client.test("Ordering demo task created", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 201, "Expected 201 Created");
    });
%}

###

### [Feature: Constraint] List tasks — observe QUERY pipeline (no AuditBehavior)
###
### Expected stdout (compare with CreateTask above — AuditBehavior is absent):
###   ▶ [metrics:10] ListTasksQuery started         ← MetricsBehavior (Order=10)
###   info: ... ListTasksQuery handled in ...       ← SimpleLoggingBehavior (runtime)
###   info: handler body (no explicit log here)
###   ◀ [metrics:10] ListTasksQuery finished        ← MetricsBehavior exit
###
### No "📝 [audit:20]" lines because ListTasksQuery does NOT implement IAuditableRequest.
### The source generator simply never emits an AuditBehavior registration for it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/tasks
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("List tasks returns 200", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 OK");
        client.assert(Array.isArray(response.body), "Response is an array");
    });
%}

###

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Scenario 3: Short-circuiting (AuthorizationBehavior)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### First: complete task A so there is something to purge
POST {{baseUrl}}/tasks/{{pbTaskIdA}}/complete

> {%
    client.test("Task A completed", function () {
        client.assert(response.status < 400, "Expected success");
    });
%}

###

### [Feature: Short-circuit] Purge WITHOUT permission — pipeline halts at AuthorizationBehavior
###
### Expect: 401 Unauthorized (typed UnauthorizedFailure → DefaultFailureHttpMapper)
###
### Expected stdout (handler 🧹 PURGING line is ABSENT):
###   ▶ [metrics:10] PurgeCompletedTasksCommand started
###   info: ... PurgeCompletedTasksCommand handled in ... with error ...  ← SimpleLogging (failure)
###   🚫 [auth] PurgeCompletedTasksCommand denied — requires 'tasks:admin'
###   ◀ [metrics:10] PurgeCompletedTasksCommand finished
###
### AuthorizationBehavior returns Result.FailUnauthorized (typed) without calling next() —
### the handler (and its "🧹 PURGING" log) never executes.
POST {{baseUrl}}/tasks/admin/purge

> {%
    client.test("Purge without permission is rejected", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 401, "Expected 401 — AuthorizationBehavior returned a typed UnauthorizedFailure");
    });
%}

###

### Verify task A still exists (not purged because the request was denied)
GET {{baseUrl}}/tasks/{{pbTaskIdA}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("Completed task A survives rejected purge", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Task A should still exist");
        client.assert(response.body.status === "Completed", "Status is still Completed");
    });
%}

###

### [Feature: Short-circuit pass-through] Purge WITH permission — pipeline continues to handler
###
### Add X-User-Permissions: tasks:admin header to satisfy AuthorizationBehavior.
### Expect: success, returns count of purged tasks (should be 1 — task A).
### Note: PurgeCompletedTasksCommand : IRequest<int> — the body is a BARE value type (int), the
### known-issue 001 regression case. It flows through the generated closed CqrsBoundaryEnforcementBehavior<,int>
### and AuthorizationBehavior<,int> (no open-generic value-type DI resolution failure under AOT).
###
### Expected stdout (compare with the denied request above — handler NOW executes):
###   ▶ [metrics:10] PurgeCompletedTasksCommand started
###   ✅ [auth] PurgeCompletedTasksCommand authorized for 'tasks:admin'
###   🧹 PURGING all completed tasks...             ← handler executing
###   🧹 PURGED 1 completed task(s)
###   info: ... PurgeCompletedTasksCommand handled in ...  ← SimpleLogging (success)
###   ◀ [metrics:10] PurgeCompletedTasksCommand finished
POST {{baseUrl}}/tasks/admin/purge
X-User-Permissions: tasks:admin

> {%
    client.test("Authorized purge returns success", function () {
        client.assert(response.status < 400, "Expected success status code");
    });

    client.test("Purge returned an int count", function () {
        client.assert(typeof response.body === "number", "Response body is a bare int (value-type response)");
        client.assert(response.body >= 1, "At least 1 task was purged");
    });
%}

###

### Verify task A is gone (purged)
GET {{baseUrl}}/tasks/{{pbTaskIdA}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("Task A was purged", function () {
        client.assert(response.status >= 400, "Task A should be gone after purge");
    });
%}

###

### Verify task B still exists (it was Pending, not Completed — not purged)
GET {{baseUrl}}/tasks/{{pbTaskIdB}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("Task B (Pending) was NOT purged", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Task B should still exist");
        client.assert(response.body.status === "Pending", "Status is still Pending");
    });
%}

###

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Scenario 4: Stream pipeline behavior (quick reference)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### [Feature: Stream behavior] Stream remaining tasks
### Observe stdout AFTER all items are yielded:
###   "🔢 Streamed N StreamTasksQuery item(s) in <elapsed>"
### See Http/streaming.http for the full streaming demo.
GET {{baseUrl}}/tasks/stream
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("Stream returns 200 with array body", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 OK");
        client.assert(Array.isArray(response.body), "Response is an array");
    });
%}

###

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Cleanup
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Delete task B
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/tasks/{{pbTaskIdB}}

> {%
    client.test("Cleanup — task B deleted", function () {
        client.assert(response.status < 400, "Expected success");
    });
%}

###
